# .privacy-words for a FRESH CLONE (this file is normally git-ignored, so it does not travel
# with the repository). Copy it to .privacy-words in your checkout and edit it to name the
# people, hosts and project names that must never appear in a public export:
#
#   cp packaging/privacy-words.example .privacy-words
#
# One regex per line; comments (#) and blank lines are skipped. A plain word shorter than 5
# letters matches as a whole word only; everything else matches anywhere, case-insensitively.
# The check already catches generic patterns without this file: absolute home paths, IP
# addresses (except loopback, 0.0.0.0 and the documentation ranges - it also flags the
# carrier-grade NAT ranges tailnets use), e-mail addresses (except example.com / GitHub
# noreply), and credentials (API keys, tokens, private keys). What it CANNOT know without
# this file is your host names, user names and project names. UNCOMMENT the lines below and
# edit them - they are EXAMPLES, not a default:
#
#   zebrahost77        a made-up host name (matches anywhere)
#   myuser             a user name (4 letters: matches as a whole word only)
#   \bproj-\w+         a project prefix, as a regex
zebrahost77
myuser
\bproj-\w+

